-------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPersisted VS Code threads fail to resume with \"list_turns is not supported yet\" · Issue #45703 · openai/codex · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/45703)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; Open VS Code on Windows 11 with the OpenAI Codex extension. ...     codex resume <THREAD_UUID> ... `codex doctor` reports that `state_5.sqlite` exists and has valid integrity, and the affected threads and their rollout files are still present locally.\n\nThe affected threads are still present locally:\n\n  * state_5.sqlite exists and codex doctor reports integrity OK\n  * the VS Code threads are present in the database\n  * the corresponding rollout .jsonl files are still present on disk\n  * codex resume --all correctly lists my older persisted threads\n\nHowever, trying to resume some of them with: `codex resume <UUID>`\n\ncorrectly resolves the rollout file, then fails with: \"thread/resume failed: list_turns is not supported yet (code -32601)\"\n\nFor another persisted VS Code thread, I instead get: \"Error: failed to load a bounded thread history page\"\n\nI also tested older Codex versions, but the affected threads still could not be resumed. I have not modified the SQLite DB or rollout files.\n\nSo in my case the threads are discoverable and indexed correctly; the failure occurs specifically when Codex tries to load/resume their history.\n\n### What steps can reproduce the bug?\n\n  1. Open VS Code on Windows 11 with the OpenAI Codex extension.\n\n  2. Open the Codex chat history and select an existing persisted conversation that previously worked normally.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCodex Desktop local thread can be hidden/stale when state_5.sqlite and session_index.jsonl drift on Windows · Issue #22452 · openai/codex · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/22452)\ncitetu